# p_4b7a — Tablet at the Counter, Not the Kitchen Table (fork of p_9c2e)

This fork corrects the single fatal error in p_9c2e: the tablet is NOT at the kitchen table. The parent's tablet claim has now accumulated 14 "against" sightings (0 for) and the kitchen_table_k1 block failed at 0.04 on 29 sightings. The per-object statistics are unambiguous: tablet_mara is at counter_k1 on 14 of 20 sighted days, and during weekday 9–17h the robot found it at the counter 9 times versus 6 empty looks. The kitchen table is where the laptop, notebook, and charger live (and where they go out from), but the tablet is a separate device that Mara leaves on the counter—likely as a reference screen for recipes, messages, or a second display after work.

What changed from p_9c2e: (1) tablet_mara's default is now counter_k1 (usually) instead of kitchen_table_k1. (2) Removed the coffee_table_l1 weekday 6–8.5 block (no evidence for morning coffee-table use). (3) Removed the 14–18h counter_k1 block (redundant when the default is already the counter). (4) Retained the 19–22h couch block for evening entertainment. (5) The tablet claim now expects counter_k1 during weekday 9–14. All other targets (glasses, phone, keys, laptop, charger, etc.) and the entry-hook/entry-table structure for jacket and wallet are unchanged from p_9c2e.

What sets this apart from p_a4f7: this document keeps the jacket at entry_hook_e1 and the wallet at entry_table_e1 with NO evening chair blocks, whereas p_a4f7 adds chair_k1/chair_k2 blocks at 17–23h. What sets this apart from p_8f2d: same distinction (no chair blocks). What would refute it: the tablet found at the kitchen table or coffee table during weekday 9–14, or the tablet found out of the house during that window.

